---
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /
is_home_page: true
date_updated: 2026-10-07
sitemap:
  changefreq: monthly
scripts:
  - /assets/js/levels.js
  - /assets/js/calculator.js
---
<div class="hero">
    <div>
        <h1>Who Wrote the Code?</h1>
        <p class="lede">Three simple characters that tell a reader who maintains a project, how much of its code is written by AI, and how closely a human checks the AI's work.</p>
        <p class="sub">Put the rating in an <code>{{ meta.file_name }}</code> file at the root of your repo. Readers decide what it means for them, and the rating doesn't judge whether using AI is good or bad.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="plate" aria-label="Example rating A2b">
        <div class="plate-head">
            <span class="label">{{ meta.site_title }}</span>
            <span class="label">Example</span>
        </div>
        <div class="plate-code">
            <div class="c1"><div class="ch">A</div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">Maintainer</div><div class="val">Expert</div></div>
            <div class="c2"><div class="ch">2</div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">AI Share</div><div class="val">26–50% of code</div></div>
            <div class="c3"><div class="ch">b</div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">Oversight</div><div class="val">Reviewed before merge</div></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<section id="levels">
    <div style="display:grid;gap:10px;">
        <span class="label">The Levels</span>
        <h2>Reading a Rating</h2>
        <p class="prose muted">Each position uses a different kind of symbol, so a rating can't be misread: an uppercase letter, then a digit, then a lowercase letter. Most other AI disclosure standards use a single level; keeping these three questions apart is what makes AI Code Rating different. <a href="{{ meta.url_related }}">See how it compares</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="positions">
        <article class="pos">
            <div class="pos-head c1"><span class="pos-num">1</span><h3>Maintainer Expertise</h3></div>
            <p class="q">Rate the least experienced person who can approve a change on their own, not every contributor. If every change needs more than one approval, rate the most experienced approval each change is guaranteed to get. Ask: could they have written this without AI, and can they explain every line?</p>
            <ul class="levels">
                {% for d in levels.maintainer %}
                <li><span class="k c1">{{ d.k }}</span><span><b>{{ d.name }}</b> {{ d.desc }}</span></li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="pos">
            <div class="pos-head c2"><span class="pos-num">2</span><h3>AI Share</h3></div>
            <p class="q">How much of the code, by lines, was generated by AI instead of typed by a person. Single-word or single-line autocomplete doesn't count. Accepted multi-line suggestions do.</p>
            <ul class="levels">
                {% for s in levels.share %}
                <li><span class="k c2">{{ s.k }}</span><span><b>{{ s.name }}</b> {{ s.desc }}</span></li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="pos">
            <div class="pos-head c3"><span class="pos-num">3</span><h3>Oversight</h3></div>
            <p class="q">How carefully a person checked AI-written code before it was merged. AI review doesn't count. Projects with no AI code use <code>a</code>, since nothing was left unchecked.</p>
            <ul class="levels">
                {% for o in levels.oversight %}
                <li><span class="k c3">{{ o.k }}</span><span><b>{{ o.name }}</b> {{ o.desc }}</span></li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="rated">
    <div style="display:grid;gap:10px;">
        <span class="label">Adopters</span>
        <h2>Rated Projects</h2>
        <p class="prose muted">Real projects that publish a rating in <code>{{ meta.file_name }}</code>. For common setups across the whole range and the reasoning behind each rating, see the <a href="{{ meta.url_examples }}">rating examples</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="examples rated">
        {% for p in ratings %}
        {% set code = own_rating.rating if p.self else p.rating %}
        <div class="ex"><a class="code" href="{{ p.acr_url }}" aria-label="{{ p.name }}'s {{ meta.file_name }}: {{ code }}"><span class="c1">{{ code[0] }}</span><span class="c2">{{ code[1] }}</span><span class="c3">{{ code[2] }}</span></a><h3><a href="{{ p.url }}">{{ p.name }}</a></h3><p>{{ p.desc }}</p></div>
        {% endfor %}
        <div class="ex ex-you"><span class="code" aria-hidden="true"><span class="c1">?</span><span class="c2">?</span><span class="c3">?</span></span><h3>Your Project</h3><p><a href="#rate">Rate your project</a>, add <code>{{ meta.file_name }}</code> to its repo, then <a href="{{ meta.url_issue_rated }}">ask to be listed</a>.</p></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="rate">
    <div style="display:grid;gap:10px;">
        <span class="label">Rate Your Project</span>
        <h2>Get Your Rating</h2>
        <p class="prose muted">Answer for the project as it stands today. The form starts with an example. Change any answer and the rating, badge and file update. No sign-up: the form runs in your browser.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="calc">
        <form id="rate-form" autocomplete="off">
            <fieldset class="q-block" data-pos="1">
                <legend><span class="pos-num">1</span><span class="c1">Who approves and merges the code?</span></legend>
                <div class="opts" id="opts-1"></div>
            </fieldset>
            <fieldset class="q-block" data-pos="2">
                <legend><span class="pos-num">2</span><span class="c2">How much of the code did AI write?</span></legend>
                <div class="opts" id="opts-2"></div>
            </fieldset>
            <fieldset class="q-block" data-pos="3">
                <legend><span class="pos-num">3</span><span class="c3">How was AI-written code checked?</span></legend>
                <div class="opts" id="opts-3"></div>
            </fieldset>
            <div class="q-block q-usage">
                <label for="usage" class="usage-label">How is AI used in this project?</label>
                <textarea id="usage" rows="5" placeholder="In plain English, a short paragraph of context for your readers."></textarea>
            </div>
            <noscript><p class="muted">The rating form needs JavaScript. You can work out your rating from the tables above.</p></noscript>
        </form>

        <div class="result">
            <div class="plate" aria-live="polite">
                <div class="plate-head">
                    <span class="label">Your Rating</span>
                    <span class="label mono" id="out-code-small"></span>
                </div>
                <div class="plate-code">
                    <div class="c1"><div class="ch" id="ch1"></div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">Maintainer</div><div class="val" id="val1"></div></div>
                    <div class="c2"><div class="ch" id="ch2"></div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">AI Share</div><div class="val" id="val2"></div></div>
                    <div class="c3"><div class="ch" id="ch3"></div><div class="tick"></div><div class="what">Oversight</div><div class="val" id="val3"></div></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="calc-note" id="calc-note" hidden></p>

            <div class="out">
                <div class="out-head"><span class="label">README Badge</span><span class="out-tools"><span class="fmt" role="group" aria-label="Badge format"><button type="button" data-fmt="md" aria-pressed="true">Markdown</button><button type="button" data-fmt="html" aria-pressed="false">HTML</button><button type="button" data-fmt="rst" aria-pressed="false">reST</button></span><button class="copy" type="button" data-target="out-badge">Copy</button></span></div>
                <div class="badge-preview" id="badge-img"></div>
                <pre id="out-badge"></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="out">
                <div class="out-head"><span class="label">{{ meta.file_name }}</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-target="out-file">Copy</button></div>
                <pre id="out-file"></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="out-next muted">Save it as <code>{{ meta.file_name }}</code> in the root of your repo and check it with the <a href="{{ meta.url_validator }}">validator</a>. To check it on every push, add the <a href="{{ meta.url_action }}">GitHub Action</a>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="file">
    <div class="two">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:16px;">
            <span class="label">The File</span>
            <h2>{{ meta.file_name }}</h2>
            <p class="prose">Put the file in the repo root. The front matter at the top is for tools like badge services, linters and code hosts to read. Below it, a short paragraph in plain English gives people a little context on how AI is used in the project.</p>
            <p class="prose muted">The name is deliberately different from <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Those files give instructions <em>to</em> AI tools. This one tells people <em>how</em> AI was used. The <a href="{{ meta.url_spec }}">full spec</a> has the details, the <a href="{{ meta.url_validator }}">validator</a> checks your file, and the <a href="{{ meta.url_action }}">GitHub Action</a> checks it on every push.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="fields">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>rating</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>The three-character rating, such as <code>A2b</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>The spec version the rating follows. Currently <code>{{ meta.spec_version }}</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>updated</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>The date the rating was last checked, as YYYY-MM-DD.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="rules">
    <div style="display:grid;gap:10px;">
        <span class="label">Rules</span>
        <h2>Using the Rating</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="rules two">
        <li><h3>Rate Accurately, Even When the Result Looks Worse</h3><p>A rating is only useful if readers trust it. An honest <code>D4c</code> tells people more than an inflated <code>B4b</code>.</p></li>
        <li><h3>AI Share Is Not a Score</h3><p>An <code>A3a</code> project can be stronger than a <code>C1e</code> one. The other two positions tell readers how much to trust the code.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Rate the People Who Merge</h3><p>Contributors come and go. The people who approve merges are responsible for what ships, so position 1 describes them.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Keep It Current</h3><p>Projects change. Check the rating when the way you work changes, and update the <code>updated</code> date when you do.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Rate the Whole Project</h3><p>One rating covers the whole repository. If a part was built differently, such as one package in a monorepo, say so in the text of the file.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Explain in Your Own Words</h3><p>Use the text of the file to add what three characters can't, such as which parts AI wrote and how you test.</p></li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="roadmap">
    <div style="display:grid;gap:10px;">
        <span class="label">Roadmap</span>
        <h2>From a File to a Standard</h2>
        <p class="prose muted">GitHub started recognizing files like <code>SECURITY.md</code> and <code>FUNDING.yml</code> after many projects already used them. This plan follows the same path.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="road">
        <div class="step"><span class="label">1. Spec and Site</span><p>Publish the spec, the rating form and the file format. Collect feedback on the draft.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><span class="label">2. Tooling</span><p>A <a href="{{ meta.url_action }}">GitHub Action</a> that checks <code>{{ meta.file_name }}</code> on every push, and badges for Markdown, HTML and reStructuredText READMEs.</p></div>
        <div class="step now"><span class="label">3. Adoption</span><p>Get well-known projects to publish ratings, and list them here.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><span class="label">4. Platform Support</span><p>Propose that GitHub and other code hosts show the rating on the repo page.</p></div>
    </div>
</section>
